  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1200 PM CDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nSurface observations outlined the strongest cold front so far\nthis young fall season, bisecting SW KS, with northeast winds\ngusting 30-40 mph behind it. The cold front, readily apparent\non radar imagery, will pass DDC through the noon hour, and will\nexit Barber county no later than 7 pm. Scattered thunderstorms\nare expected to develop along the advancing cold front's\nconvergence axis, but with the front's rapid movement, any\nstorms would only impact the southeast counties (southeast of a\nColdwater-Pratt line) for a few hours 4-8 pm. Wind shear and\ninstability will both be modest at best, but just like\nyesterday, some loosely organized clusters may support wind\ngusts of 50-70 mph. SPC maintains its marginal 5% wind\nprobability for these zones. Elsewhere, cold advection will\nestablish this afternoon, with lowering stratus clouds and\nfalling afternoon temperatures. \n\nLow stratus clouds will prevail tonight, with 1023 mb cool\nsurface ridging quickly building into SW KS by 7 am Wed. Sunrise\ntemperatures Wednesday will be closer to mid September normals,\nin the upper 50s and lower 60s. At least scattered showers and\nthunderstorms are probable tonight into early Wednesday, as warm\nair advection aloft quickly establishes above the cool boundary\nlayer. \n\nResidual frontal boundary retreats rapidly northward as a warm\nfront Wednesday, synoptically encouraged by a strong trough\narriving in the Great Basin, and a powerful 600 dm ridge\ncentered over the Mississippi valley. South winds return quickly\nWednesday, allowing for temperatures to rebound. MOS guidance\nforecasts afternoon temperatures in the 80s, while NBM is warmer\ninto the lower 90s. Kept the warmer guidance as subsidence and\nrising heights increase from the east. This will also keep most\nof SW KS dry, with any thunderstorms near Goodland Wednesday\nevening. \n\nThe unseasonable heat reestablishes fully Thursday, as the\npowerful ridge over the SE US begins retrograding westward.\nStronger south winds, stronger than NBM guidance, will\nfacilitate mixing and encourage temperatures to easily warm\nthrough the 90s. Some locations will approach 100, yet again. \nThe heat is expected to peak Friday, with afternoon temperatures\nof 95-100 common, and some record highs possible. \n\nShortwave trough over the northern Rockies Friday, will advance\neastward into the northern plains Sunday. This process will send\nthe next cold front down the plains, reducing afternoon\ntemperatures to the 70s Sunday and Monday. Showers and\nthunderstorms will accompany this cold front, with NBM currently\nhighlighting likely category pops centered on Saturday night.",
